WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.280 --> 00:00:04.090
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.090 --> 00:00:09.500
Earlier, we talked about an amazing technique
where the inputs were a source video of ourselves,

00:00:09.500 --> 00:00:10.990
and a target actor.

00:00:10.990 --> 00:00:14.559
And the output was a
reenactment, in other words, a video of this

00:00:14.559 --> 00:00:17.040
target actor with our facial gestures.

00:00:17.040 --> 00:00:21.740
This requires only a few minutes of video
from the target which is usually already available

00:00:21.740 --> 00:00:22.940
on the internet.

00:00:22.940 --> 00:00:27.140
Essentially, we can impersonate other people,
at least for one video.

00:00:27.140 --> 00:00:32.070
A key part of this new technique is that it
extracts additional data such as pose and

00:00:32.070 --> 00:00:38.079
eye positions both from the source and target
videos, and uses this data for the reconstruction.

00:00:38.079 --> 00:00:43.449
As opposed to this original Face2face technique
from 2 years ago, which was already mind-blowing,

00:00:43.449 --> 00:00:48.910
you see here that this results in a new learning-based
method that supports the reenactment of eyebrows

00:00:48.910 --> 00:00:54.360
and blinking, changing the background, plus
head and gaze positioning as well.

00:00:54.360 --> 00:00:58.370
So far, this would still be similar to a non
learning-based technique we've seen a few

00:00:58.370 --> 00:00:59.659
episodes ago.

00:00:59.659 --> 00:01:05.030
And now, hold on to your papers, because this
algorithm enables us to not only impersonate,

00:01:05.030 --> 00:01:08.400
but also control the characters in the output
video.

00:01:08.400 --> 00:01:13.390
The results are truly mesmerizing, I almost
fell out of the chair when I've first seen

00:01:13.390 --> 00:01:14.390
them.

00:01:14.390 --> 00:01:19.619
And what's more, we can create really rich
reenactments by editing the expressions, pose,

00:01:19.619 --> 00:01:22.140
and blinking separately by hand.

00:01:22.140 --> 00:01:26.700
What also needs to be emphasized here is that
we see and talk to other human beings all

00:01:26.700 --> 00:01:30.649
the time, so we have a remarkably keen eye
for these kinds of gestures.

00:01:30.649 --> 00:01:36.009
If something is off by just a few millimeters
or is not animated in a way that is close

00:01:36.009 --> 00:01:39.689
to perfect, the illusion immediately falls
apart.

00:01:39.689 --> 00:01:44.090
And the magical thing about these techniques
is that every single iteration we get something

00:01:44.090 --> 00:01:47.470
that is way beyond the capabilities of the
previous methods.

00:01:47.470 --> 00:01:49.100
And they come in quick succession.

00:01:49.100 --> 00:01:53.470
There are plenty of more comparisons in the
paper as well, so make sure to have a look.

00:01:53.470 --> 00:01:59.289
It also contains a great idea that opens up
the possibility of creating quantitative evaluations

00:01:59.289 --> 00:02:00.940
against ground truth footage.

00:02:00.940 --> 00:02:04.469
Turns out that we can have such a thing as
ground truth footage.

00:02:04.469 --> 00:02:08.929
I wonder when we will see the first movie
with this kind of reenactment of an actor

00:02:08.929 --> 00:02:09.929
who passed away.

00:02:09.929 --> 00:02:12.340
Do you have some other cool applications in
mind?

00:02:12.340 --> 00:02:14.069
Let me know in the comments section.

00:02:14.069 --> 00:02:18.349
And, if you enjoyed this episode, make sure
to pick up some cool perks on our Patreon

00:02:18.349 --> 00:02:23.069
page where you can manage your paper addiction
by getting early access to these episodes

00:02:23.069 --> 00:02:24.069
and more.

